    "innovation": "This paper presents a new 4D-Var inversion system for atmospheric CO2 that combines an icosahedral grid model with a novel optimization scheme (POpULar), which simplifies the inclusion of prior error correlations to significantly improve surface flux estimations.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "NICAM-TM 4D-Var v1.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "NICAM-TM",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "In developing our 4D-Var inversion system, we have introduced unique numerical techniques that have not been used in previous 4D-Var inversions of GHGs; these techniques use an icosahedral grid transport model based on the Non-hydrostatic Icosahedral Atmospheric Model (NICAM; Tomita and Satoh, 2004; Satoh et al., 2008, 2014), along with an efficient optimization scheme of Preconditioned Optimiz- ing Utility for Large-dimensional analyses (POpULar; Fujii and Kamachi, 2003; Fujii, 2005). NICAM is one of the most advanced general circulation models (GCMs), with its dynamical frame structured with quasi-homogenous grids that are made by recursively dividing an icosahedron, which is completely different from the regular latitude-longitude grid models used so far. For the new inversion system, we have employed the NICAM-based Transport Model (NICAMTM), naming our inversion system as NICAM-TM 4D-Var. The previous accompanying paper of Niwa et al. (2017) derived and evaluated the offline forward and adjoint models of NICAM-TM. This paper describes the entire NICAM-TM 4D-Var system, including the optimization scheme POpULar.",
